## No Commercial Use

You may not use the software, or anything built with it, for any
commercial use. Commercial use is never a permitted purpose. This
applies to everyone, including the organizations described under
Noncommercial Organizations.

**Commercial use** means any use intended for, or directed toward,
commercial advantage or monetary compensation, by you or anyone else.
Commercial use includes, without limitation:

1. use by or on behalf of a business, sole proprietorship, or
   self-employed person in the course of its business, including
   its internal operations;
2. building, hosting, maintaining, or customizing websites,
   applications, or services for clients or employers, or for anyone
   else in exchange for payment or any other consideration;
3. offering the software, or functionality substantially derived from
   it, as a hosted service, platform, or product;
4. selling, licensing, renting, or otherwise charging for the
   software, changed versions of it, or access to either; and
5. operating a website or service built with the software that sells
   goods or services, shows paid advertising, sponsorship, or
   affiliate links, takes paid subscriptions or donations in exchange
   for content or features, or otherwise generates revenue.

If you are unsure whether a use is commercial, treat it as commercial
until the licensor confirms otherwise in writing. Commercial licenses
may be available from the licensor on request through
https://github.com/dev-geddy.

## No Copies or Replicas

You may make only the copies you need to use the software for a
permitted purpose on systems you control, such as local clones,
builds, backups, and deployments of your own permitted website. You
may not make or share any other copies. In particular, you may not:

1. publish, mirror, re-host, upload, or otherwise distribute the
   software or any part of it, original or changed, in source or
   compiled form, including as a template, starter kit, boilerplate,
   theme, package, library, or repository, whether publicly or with
   any other person;
2. create, publish, or offer a replica of the software. A
   **replica** is any software, template, theme, design, or service
   that reproduces or imitates the software's code, structure,
   features, user interface, design, or documentation closely enough
   to substitute for it, whether made by copying, adapting,
   translating, or porting the software, or by using the software or
   its source code as a reference or as input to an automated or
   AI-assisted tool;
3. use the software or its source code to train, fine-tune, or
   evaluate a machine-learning model, or as retrieval material for
   one, other than for a permitted purpose that does not produce a
   replica; or
4. remove, obscure, or alter any license, copyright, or attribution
   notice in the software.

